===Etymology===
From {{etyl|gem-pro|ang}} {{m|gem-pro|*deuzą}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|ang}} {{m|ine-pro|*dʰewsóm}}.

Germanic cognates include {{etyl|osx|-}} {{m|osx|dior}}, {{etyl|nl|-}} {{m|nl|dier}}, {{etyl|goh|-}} {{m|goh|tior}} ({{etyl|de|-}} {{m|de|Tier}}), {{etyl|non|-}} {{m|non|dýr}} ({{etyl|da|-}} {{m|da|dyr}}, {{etyl|sv|-}} {{m|sv|djur}}), {{etyl|got|-}} {{m|got|𐌳𐌹𐌿𐍃}}. The Indo-European root also led to {{etyl|lt|-}} {{m|lt|dvēsti}}, {{etyl|lv|-}} {{m|lv|dvēsele}}, {{etyl|sla-pro|-}} {{m|sla-pro|*duša}} ({{etyl|ru|-}} {{m|ru|душа}}).

